exports[`the Markdown of a real page > of the help centre 1`] = `
"---
title: "Using Tabs | livediagram"
description: "Split your work into tabs: add, name, reorder, delete and switch tabs on the tab bar, with every action in the tab menu."
url: "https://www.liveworkspace.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/"
---

[Tabs](/help/tabs/)

# Tabs

One piece of work, many tabs. Tabs let you split a project across several parts, each with its own content, theme, and background.

Applies To

livediagram, liveplanner, livedocuments, livedesign and livehuddle

A canvas is a collection of **tabs**. A new canvas usually starts with one tab, and you add more whenever a piece of work deserves its own space. The tab bar sits along the bottom of the editor, and the active tab fills the area above it.

In livediagram and livehuddle

A canvas is not a single page: each tab has its own elements, layout, theme, and background.

## What a tab is

Each tab is a self-contained canvas inside the canvas. A tab carries its own elements, its own theme and background pattern, and an optional lock, all independent of the other tabs around it. Common ways people split work across tabs:

- An **Overview** tab with the high-level picture, and detail tabs behind it.
- One tab per system, team, or phase of a project.
- A scratch tab for ideas you are not ready to file yet.

In livediagram

Each tab also has its own [mode](/help/canvas/editor-modes/) (Diagram, Draw or Workshop), the same for everyone on it, chosen on its start while it is empty. The small icon at the start of a tab's name shows it.

The tab bar runs along the bottom of the editor: the active tab is raised with its ⋯ menu button, and the plus button adds another tab.

## Adding a tab

Press the **+** button at the end of the tab bar, or run **Create New Tab** from the [command palette](/help/search-panel/the-search-panel/command-palette/) (\`Cmd\` / \`Ctrl\` + \`K\`). The new tab:

- is named \`Tab 2\`, \`Tab 3\` and so on, after the number of tabs in the canvas;
- takes the theme, background and font of the tab you were on, so it matches the work around it;
- starts on its own start, so you can begin from a template or close it to keep the tab blank.

In livediagram

It opens in the same mode as the tab you were on: made from a Workshop tab it is a Workshop tab, and from a Draw tab a Draw tab. It starts on that mode's own start, such as **Start Drawing** or **Start a Workshop**.

**Duplicate** in the tab menu makes a copy instead: it is named after the original with "copy" on the end, sits right after it, and is a separate tab with its own content from then on.

## Naming and reordering tabs

Tabs start with default names like \`Tab 1\` and \`Tab 2\`, and you can rename any of them:

- **Double-click** the active tab's name to edit it inline. Enter or clicking away saves, Escape cancels, and an empty name keeps the old one.
- **Rename** in the tab menu (and **Rename Tab** in the command palette) opens the same editor.

In livediagram, livedocuments, livedesign and livehuddle

**Auto-rename on first edit:** while a tab still has its default name, editing the label of its first element renames the tab to match. This only happens once, so later edits leave a renamed tab alone.

Tab names are capped at **60 characters**. Runs of spaces and line breaks collapse to one, and anything longer is cut at the last whole word with an ellipsis, so a paragraph typed into a sticky can't turn into an unreadable tab.

Drag a tab left or right to reorder it. A blue bar shows where it will land: drop on the left half of a tab to land before it, the right half to land after it. Reordering only moves the tab in the list, it never touches the content on any tab.

Drag a tab up off the bar to the **right edge** of the screen instead, and it opens beside the tab you're on: see [Side by Side Tabs](/help/tabs/side-by-side/).

Double-click the active tab's name to edit it inline, then press Enter to save or Escape to cancel.

## The tab menu

Click the **⋯** button on the active tab, or right-click any tab (which switches to it first), to open its menu. On a phone it opens as a sheet from the bottom of the screen.

In livediagram, livedocuments, livedesign and livehuddle

Right-clicking an empty spot on the canvas opens the same menu, with **Paste** moved to a row of its own at the top.

Along the top sits a row of quick actions: **Rename**, **Duplicate**, **Paste** (greyed out until you have copied something), **Side by Side** (on a large enough window; **Close Side by Side** while two tabs are open), **Lock Tab** and **Delete**. Below them, everything else is a full-width row, in groups split by a line:

- **Organise in Tab Folder** and **Add to Another Canvas**.
- **Import**, **Export**, and **Clear**, which removes every element from the tab after a confirmation (undo brings them back; it is greyed out on an empty tab).

In livediagram, livedocuments, livedesign and livehuddle

To tidy a tab's layout, search for **Auto Layout** or **Auto-align** in the [command palette](/help/search-panel/the-search-panel/command-palette/). See [Cleaning Up a Tab](/help/tabs/tab-cleanup/).

In livediagram

In Diagram mode you can also press **Tidy** in the bottom-right corner. The [session tools](/help/collaboration/session-tools/) (timer, vote and poll) are not in this menu: they have their own buttons in the bottom bar.

In livediagram

The tab menu: quick actions along the top, then full-width rows for folders and canvases, and content.

## Deleting a tab

Choose **Delete** in the tab menu. The tab goes straight away, and **Undo** brings it back. Once you reload or leave the canvas it is gone, unless another canvas also holds it, so export or duplicate anything you might want later. A few things to know:

- A canvas always keeps at least one tab, so Delete is greyed out on the last one.
- Links on other tabs that pointed at the deleted tab are removed with it.
- If the tab is also in another canvas, it is only removed from this one.

In livediagram, liveplanner, livedocuments and livedesign

A [locked tab](/help/tabs/locking-tabs/) can't be deleted until you unlock it.

## Share links

On a share link for a Viewer or a Participant, and on any link scoped to one tab, you can still click between tabs, but the **+** button, the tab menu, renaming and drag-to-reorder are all hidden. Only an Editor with a link to the whole canvas changes its tabs.

## More with tabs

Each tab also carries its own menu of actions, covered in their own guides:

- **[Tab Folders](/help/tabs/tab-folders/)** group related tabs under one named chip so a long tab bar stays readable.
- **[Side by Side Tabs](/help/tabs/side-by-side/)** puts two tabs on screen at once, each live, with the toolbars following you between them.
- **[Connected Files](/help/tabs/connected-files/)** adds a tab that opens a file from any liveworkspace app: a plan, a document, a design or another canvas.
- **[Importing](/help/tabs/import-tabs/)** and **[Exporting](/help/tabs/export-tabs/)** move a tab's contents in and out as a file.

In livediagram, livedocuments, livedesign and livehuddle

**[Cleaning Up a Tab](/help/tabs/tab-cleanup/)** tidies a tab's layout in one click.

In livediagram and livedesign

**[Linking Across Tabs](/help/tabs/linking-tabs/)** turns any element into a jump point to another tab.

In livediagram, livedocuments and livehuddle

**[Add a Tab to Another Canvas ](/help/tabs/add-to-document/)**&#x73;hares a tab into a different canvas: it is the same tab in both places, not a copy, so edits appear everywhere it lives.

In livediagram, liveplanner, livedocuments and livedesign

**[Locking a Tab](/help/tabs/locking-tabs/)** makes a whole tab read-only.

A shared reference tab such as a glossary can live in more than one canvas at once with Add to Another Canvas. Editing it in one place updates it everywhere.

**How this works in 4 other apps**

In liveplanner

Each tab holds exactly one thing: a **Board** of cards in columns, a **Sheet** of cells and formulas, a **Dashboard** of metrics and charts, or a **Form** whose answers become cards.

In liveplanner

Common ways to split a plan across tabs: a **Board** for the day-to-day work and a **Dashboard** beside it that sums it up; a **Sheet** for the budget or the numbers behind the plan; a **Form** for requests, whose answers land as cards on a board. The cards on a plan's boards belong to the plan rather than to one tab, so two boards can show the same cards, and a Dashboard can chart them. See [Planning Basics](/help/plan/planning-basics/). The small icon at the start of a tab's name shows what it holds.

In liveplanner

It takes the look of the tab you were on, and opens on **Start Planning**, where you pick what it holds: a board, sheet, form, visualisation or dashboard, from a template or blank. Picking one usually names the tab too, such as "Kanban" for a Kanban board.

In liveplanner

There is no row for adding the tab to another plan: a tab belongs to one plan (see below). **Import…** opens the [Import dialog](/help/tabs/import-tabs/): a CSV into a Board or Sheet tab, a tab's JSON, or a board from Trello, Asana, Jira or Linear. **Clear** empties the tab after a confirmation (Undo brings it back).

In liveplanner

The tab menu: quick actions along the top, then full-width rows for folders, and for importing, exporting and clearing.

In liveplanner

Delete asks first only when it does something Undo can't take back: a [form](/help/plan/forms/)'s tab deletes the form's link with it, and a board or sheet that forms send their responses to stops them taking responses. Links on other tabs are not mentioned in liveplanner.

In liveplanner

**Tabs in more than one plan.** A tab belongs to one plan. Tabs used to be addable to another plan from the tab menu, but a plan's tabs show its cards, and a card is kept in the plan it came from, so a tab carried into another plan couldn't take its cards with it. Tabs that were already shared between plans stay shared: an edit in either plan shows in both, and deleting the tab in one plan only removes it from that one. To reuse a tab's layout somewhere new, [export it](/help/tabs/export-tabs/) and [import it](/help/tabs/import-tabs/) into the other plan.

In liveplanner

Lots of tabs? Press \`⌘K\` and type part of a tab's name to jump straight to it, or group them into [folders](/help/tabs/tab-folders/). In liveplanner a tab belongs to one plan, so a shared reference tab as above does not apply.

In livedocuments and livedesign

Each tab has its own pages, elements, layout, theme, and background.

In livedocuments and livedesign

Each tab is a self-contained row of [pages](/help/pages/working-with-pages/) inside the canvas.

In livedocuments and livedesign

It starts on **Start Making Pages**.

In livedocuments and livedesign

**Side by Side** is offered once the canvas has another tab to pair it with. The row reads \\*\\*Add to Canvas \\*\\* rather than \\*\\*Add to Another Canvas \\*\\*.

In livedocuments

The tab menu: quick actions along the top, then full-width rows for folders and documents, and content.

In livedesign

The tab menu: quick actions along the top, then full-width rows for folders and designs, and content.

In livedesign

**[Add a Tab to Another Design](/help/tabs/add-to-design/)** shares a tab into a different design: it is the same tab in both places, not a copy, so edits appear everywhere it lives.

In livehuddle

It starts on **Start a Space**, with an [Avatar Pad](/help/palette/avatar-pads/) where people arrive.

In livehuddle

The quick actions have no **Lock Tab**, and the menu has an **[Attendance](/help/collaboration/live-presence/tab-attendance/)** row: who was in the tab, when they joined and left, and how long they stayed. It isn't offered in a space kept [Offline](/help/privacy-and-security/offline-mode/).

In livehuddle

The [session tools](/help/collaboration/session-tools/) are not in this menu: a Timer or Stopwatch is a piece you place in a room.

In livehuddle

The tab menu: quick actions along the top, then full-width rows for folders and spaces, and content.

In livehuddle

A Participant's tab menu (and that of anyone on a link scoped to one tab, other than a Viewer) holds **Attendance** alone; a Viewer gets no tab menu, and the **+** button, renaming and drag-to-reorder are hidden.

Was this article helpful?

## Still need help?

Try [Troubleshooting](/help/troubleshooting/), or tell us what you were trying to do.

[Contact us](https://www.liveplanner.app/form/90Bgj5sY9eTpVrjhx9tTZj)

### Read It In

- [livediagram Reading](https://www.livediagram.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/)
- [liveplanner](https://www.liveplanner.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/)
- [livedocuments](https://www.livedocuments.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/)
- [livedesign](https://www.livedesign.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/)
- [livehuddle](https://www.livehuddle.app/help/tabs/using-tabs/)
"
